<resources>
    <!-- ★ Remove the refresh-rate picker from Settings > Display.

         ── Why ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         On this panel every rate above 120 Hz is strictly worse, and that is
         measured, not assumed (work/s18/touch-rate/, 8 s per window, panel rate
         read back inside every window):

             panel  HighReportRate   finger      pen
             120    0                120 Hz      240 Hz  (Pen_ID 2;87)
             120    1                360 Hz      none
             144    0                185 Hz      none
             144    1                185 Hz      none

         At 144 Hz the digitizer cannot see the active pen at all, AND the finger
         report rate falls to 185 Hz — barely half of the 360 Hz available at
         120 Hz, because HighReportRate stops doing anything above 120. So 144 Hz
         costs the stylus and half the touch sampling to buy 24 extra frames.
         There is no configuration in which a user is better off there.

         The list itself cannot be filtered: the picker builds its entries at
         runtime from Display.getSupportedModes(), so no RRO can remove one entry.
         Removing the mode from the panel timings would mean patching dtbo.img —
         panel-specific, and this device has two panel suppliers whose timings
         differ, so it would break the rule that no device-tree file may hardcode
         a panel. Hiding the control is the only panel-agnostic lever.

         ── ⚠️ SESSION 21: THIS OVERLAY WAS INERT AND IS NOW REAL ────────────
         Everything above was written against PixelOS and stayed correct about
         the hardware. What it got wrong is the resource name. It overrode

             config_show_advanced_refresh_rate

         which appears EXACTLY ONCE in the whole LineageOS 23.2 tree — in this
         file. It is not a Settings resource here, and neither
         RefreshRateUtils.java nor RefreshRatePreferenceController.java exists in
         packages/apps/Settings at all. The RRO built, idmap2 warned once a boot,
         and the picker was hidden only by LineageOS's own defaults — the same
         silent-no-op failure mode this tree's SystemUI overlay documents for
         quick_settings_tiles_default.

         LineageOS 23.2 has THREE separate refresh-rate controls, each with its
         own gate, and all three already default to hidden:

           config_show_smooth_display          packages/apps/Settings/res/values/
                                               config.xml:577            -> false
             the classic Smooth display toggle (PeakRefreshRateSwitchPreference)

           config_show_peak_refresh_rate_switch  .../res/values/
                                                 lineage_config.xml:36   -> false
             LineageOS's advanced picker, PeakRefreshRateListPreferenceController
             :108-114 returns UNSUPPORTED_ON_DEVICE unless this is true

           config_show_min_refresh_rate_switch   lineage_config.xml:39   -> false
             MinRefreshRatePreferenceController :50, a LineageOS-only control
             that did not exist under PixelOS

         All three are restated below rather than left to upstream. Depending on
         an upstream default is not the same as stating a device requirement: if
         LineageOS ever flips one, the picker returns, a user selects 144 Hz, and
         the stylus stops working with nothing in this tree to explain why.

         ── ⚠️ The trap, for whoever revisits this ───────────────────────────
         The obvious "safer" variant — hide the advanced list and let the classic
         Smooth display toggle stand in — does the OPPOSITE of what it looks like
         on this build. Two facts compose badly:

           * PeakRefreshRatePreferenceController:71-73 sets its target rate from
             findHighestRefreshRateAmongAllDisplays() when
             com.android.server.display.feature.flags.
             back_up_smooth_display_and_force_peak_refresh_rate is on, and
             `adb shell aflags list` reports it ENABLED here.
           * :118-120 then writes Float.POSITIVE_INFINITY, which
             DisplayModeDirector.java:1179-1182 resolves to the highest supported
             rate.

         So that toggle would offer 60 or 144 — the one value we are removing.
         That is why config_show_smooth_display is pinned false below rather than
         left alone: it is the fallback controller's gate, and "restore the
         toggle" is the change that looks safest and is not.

         ── What the user is left with ───────────────────────────────────────
         No refresh-rate control in Settings at all. The rate is owned by
         MalbecParts (Device settings > Device mode), which pins
         peak_refresh_rate to 120 in both modes; config_defaultPeakRefreshRate is
         120 as well, so an un-provisioned device agrees.

         ⚠️ SESSION 33. This paragraph used to end *"AOSP's own idle and
         content-driven switching still runs underneath — measured 30 Hz idle,
         120 Hz while scrolling — so nothing is lost by dropping the manual 60 Hz
         entry."* The measurement was right and the conclusion was wrong: 30 Hz
         visibly FLICKERS on this LCD, and SurfaceFlinger's residency counters
         had the tablet there for 38m49s against 120 Hz's 20m11s in one session.
         MalbecParts now pins min_refresh_rate to the same ceiling and sets
         Settings.Secure.match_content_frame_rate to MATCH_CONTENT_FRAMERATE_NEVER,
         so there is no idle drop and no content-driven switching left to lose.

         That STRENGTHENS the case for hiding the pickers rather than weakening
         it: with switching off, a stray 144 in peak_refresh_rate is no longer a
         rate the panel visits occasionally, it is the rate it sits at — and the
         digitizer cannot see the pen there at all.

         MalbecParts keeps its ContentObserver on peak_refresh_rate and
         min_refresh_rate anyway. This removes the UI, not the setting: `settings
         put`, a backup restore, or a POSITIVE_INFINITY left by the flag above can
         still move it, and the observer is what keeps /proc coherent if that
         happens. -->
    <bool name="config_show_smooth_display">false</bool>
    <bool name="config_show_peak_refresh_rate_switch">false</bool>
    <bool name="config_show_min_refresh_rate_switch">false</bool>

    <!-- ★ Remove "System update" from Settings > System.

         This port does no OTA (HANDOFF.md §2), and `Updater` is not installed —
         MalbecParts' Android.bp carries `overrides: ["Updater"]`, and
         `pm list packages | rg -c updater` reads 0 on the device. The row was
         still there, which is the whole reason this bool is needed:

         ⚠️ IT IS NOT LINEAGE'S UPDATER. The preference is AOSP's own
         (packages/apps/Settings/res/xml/system_dashboard_fragment.xml:105-114,
         key system_update_settings), and it self-removes only when nothing
         resolves android.settings.SYSTEM_UPDATE_SETTINGS
         (SystemUpdatePreferenceController.kt:47-58 via SystemUpdateRepository.kt
         :30-37). On this build **GMS resolves it** —
         com.google.android.gms.update.SystemUpdateActivity, FLAG_SYSTEM on
         /product, so MATCH_SYSTEM_ONLY matches — so removing Updater could never
         have removed the row. LineageOS 23.2's Settings has no updater-specific
         code path at all; `rg -rl org.lineageos.updater packages/apps/Settings`
         is empty.

         The bool is checked FIRST (:42-45, `AVAILABLE` iff this and isAdminUser),
         before the resolver is ever consulted, so this holds with or without GMS
         and Settings search drops the row too.

         Target resource verified to exist, because an RRO that names a resource
         the target does not have builds clean and is a SILENT NO-OP — see the
         session-21 note at the top of this file:
         packages/apps/Settings/res/values/config.xml:496 declares it, true.

         ⚠️ STATED RESIDUE. GMS ALSO injects itself into Security & privacy
         (com.android.settings.category.ia.security, keyhint
         security_status_security_patch_level — a key that exists nowhere in
         LineageOS 23.2 Settings, so it adds a row rather than replacing one).
         This bool does not gate that tile and no RRO can reach it: removing it
         needs `pm disable-user` on a component that also serves the
         SYSTEM_UPDATE_SETTINGS filter, i.e. a runtime state change rather than a
         build-time one. Not done; recorded. -->
    <bool name="config_show_system_update_settings">false</bool>
</resources>
